  "research_id": "federal-strategic-plans",
  "title": "Agency strategic plans are a legal cycle, and many still are not out",
  "question": "Which federal strategies are current, who must carry them out, and what programs, money or measured results show that they moved beyond a plan?",
  "bluf": "Federal agency strategic plans are a required, recurring cycle that ties goals to yearly plans, budgets and results reports. A stated goal is not funding, legal authority or a result, and as of September 2026 several cabinet and small-agency plans were still missing.",
  "evidence_cutoff": "2026-09-20",
  "evidence_cutoff_note": "General plan survey, lane findings and entity-chart snapshot: July 22, 2026. August 17 adds the NSSTS national functional strategy only. The Core Missions extension retains separate 392-row entity and 226-body committee denominators and 18 Wave 1 assessments. September 18 adds the live plan-instrument and EDGAR control/procurement refresh; September 20 joins the proposed whole-FAR rewrite as a surrounding procurement-control change without claiming a new EDGAR award or design. Published direction and proposed rules are not implementation or achieved effect.",
  "use_note": "Use the controlling Chronicle package for the saved legal framework, plan identities and coverage distinctions. Use the Workbench Core Missions extension for its active source-first mission and direction evidence, preserving the separate entity and committee denominators and the incomplete-wave boundary. For EDGAR, keep Commission rule/form authority, Chairman direction, EBO business ownership, OIT operation, acquisition, filer delegation, public access, deployment and compliance distinct; EDGAR disclosure is not title or settlement. Mission, authority, plan, resource, implementation, performance, independent validation, and result are separate states.",
